{{!-- A desk, on the plate: its name, its question, what it is for,
      and the story currently standing for it.

      Call from inside {{#tag}}. The feature prefers a featured post
      from this desk and falls back to the most recent one. A desk
      with nothing published keeps its name, its question and its
      purpose, and says so plainly rather than printing a broken
      grid or a count of zero. --}}

<div class="lc-wrap lc-plate-body" data-desk="{{slug}}">

    <div class="lc-deskhead">
        <div>
            <h1>{{name}}</h1>
            <p class="lc-desk-q">{{> "desk-question"}}</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lc-desk-purpose">{{#if description}}{{description}}{{else}}{{> "desk-purpose"}}{{/if}}</p>
    </div>

    {{!-- One query, ordered, rather than a featured query with a fallback
          inside it. Two reasons, both learned the hard way.

          The {{else}} of {{#get}} does not fire on an empty result set,
          so a fallback written there never runs. And a {{#get}} nested
          inside another one is evaluated in the outer query's context,
          where {{slug}} does not exist: the inner filter came out as
          "tag:" and Ghost returned nothing at all, so a desk with
          nothing featured showed neither a feature nor an empty state.

          Ordering featured first and published_at second says the same
          thing in one query that cannot go wrong: the featured story if
          there is one, otherwise the most recent. --}}
    {{#get "posts" filter="tag:{{slug}}" limit="1" order="featured desc, published_at desc" include="authors,tags"}}
        {{#if posts}}
            {{#foreach posts}}{{> "desk-feature"}}{{/foreach}}
        {{else}}
            <p class="lc-empty lc-plate-empty">Stories from this desk are forthcoming.</p>
        {{/if}}
    {{/get}}

</div>
